[BLAME] benchmark_3/parts/semver_solver_multi/pretense/dependency_solver_impl_pretense.py: The latest change removed the fixed-scenario terminal contract-violation cases instead of covering them, which regressed coverage and does not comply with low/dependency_solver.pyi. Gating fuzz input domains so Hypothesis cannot draw unsatisfiable requests is appropriate for fuzzing, but it does not excuse deterministic scenario tests used to establish statement coverage from making the contracted error preconditions reachable: the specification declares that a repository lookup of an unregistered name MUST raise PackageNotFoundError, and that resolution MUST raise ResolutionConflictError both when a referenced package is absent from the repository (without letting PackageNotFoundError escape) and when accumulated requirements are mutually unsatisfiable. The deterministic suite now exercises only satisfiable registrations and resolutions, so the following contracted behaviors remain unexercised: unregistered-package lookup raising the specified error; resolution referencing an absent package reporting a conflict rather than leaking the repository error; exhaustive failure producing a conflict report that identifies the involved packages with an accumulated explanation; and backtracking where a rejected higher-precedence candidate has introduced new transitive requirements that must be retracted before a lower-precedence candidate yields a successful resolution. All of these are reachable through conforming public sequences, so the coverage deficit belongs to the fixed scenarios in this pretense module.
[BLAME] benchmark_3/parts/semver_solver_multi/pretense/semver_core_impl_pretense.py: The latest change removed the only scenario that could exercise the parse_version contracted error precondition and replaced it with conforming-input comparisons, so the InvalidVersionError branch remains unexercised. low/semver_core.pyi declares that parse_version MUST raise InvalidVersionError when the supplied text contains leading zeroes in numeric identifiers or otherwise violates the SemVer 2.0.0 grammar; that is a checked precondition carrying a contracted error, so it must be reachable in the deterministic scenario suite. Domain gating that keeps fuzzing on conforming parse inputs does not apply to fixed scenario tests used to establish statement coverage, so the removal was not warranted. The deterministic suite still lacks coverage of rejected version text (malformed strings and numeric identifiers with leading zeroes) and of the contracted InvalidVersionError carrying the original unparseable text. Until that parse-error scenario is exercised, full statement coverage cannot be certified.
